:: Analyst Restrain [nobr] {"position":"2500,1600","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "analystRestrain">>
        You move toward the Analyst. Its response is slow. You restrain it — the alien's crystalline structure is not built for resistance. It doesn't fight. It observes.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Analyst">>
        Physical intervention. Unprompted by research protocol. Categorising."
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[I need the signal.|Analyst Restrain Need]]
            [[I'm sorry.|Analyst Restrain Sorry]]
            [[Stop categorising me and answer.|Analyst Restrain Answer]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Analyst Restrain Answer [nobr] {"position":"2700,1700","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "analystRestrainAnswer">>
        <<speaker "Analyst">>
        I am always categorising. The two are not separable. But the signal — yes. You could have asked again. The interaction on our end was concluded but not permanently closed. I would have responded to a renewed approach.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        [ SIGNAL ACQUIRED: 119.5 — 1.2 — 45.1 ]
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You could have just— you didn't need to— alright. The signal is what matters.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Exit the specimen bay.|Specimen Bay Exit]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Analyst Restrain Need [nobr] {"position":"2700,1500","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "analystRestrainNeed">>
        <<speaker "Analyst">>
        The signal was not provided because the interaction reached a conclusion on our end. You were not notified of the conclusion. I note this as a communication irregularity. The signal can be provided now. The physical intervention was not necessary but is not consequential.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        The Analyst resumes its work as if nothing happened.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        [ SIGNAL ACQUIRED: 119.5 — 1.2 — 45.1 ]
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Exit the specimen bay.|Specimen Bay Exit]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Analyst Restrain Sorry [nobr] {"position":"2700,1600","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "analystRestrainSorry">>
        <<speaker "Analyst">>
        Apology. An expression of regret for an action taken. The action was taken anyway. The apology does not reverse the action. I am noting that your species issues these expressions frequently in situations where the action was deliberate. The function of the expression is unclear.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Analyst">>
        The signal. You wanted the signal. Here.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        [ SIGNAL ACQUIRED: 119.5 — 1.2 — 45.1 ]
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Exit the specimen bay.|Specimen Bay Exit]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Analyst Strike [nobr] {"position":"2500,1900","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "analystStrike">>
        You strike the Analyst at the base of its cranial structure. The alien collapses with the stillness of a system shutting down. The yellow gas begins to leak from the fracture. You retreat.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        Among the shards: a small component. Dense. Still warm. The biochip translates its function without being asked: sensory processing unit. Signal emitter.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        [ SIGNAL ACQUIRED: 119.5 — 1.2 — 45.1 ]
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        I want you to know I'm not going to pretend that didn't just happen. It happened. You did that.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        The device will work. The signal is in it. I'm not going to tell you it was necessary because I don't know if it was.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        It'll recover. They always recover. That doesn't mean— it doesn't make it—
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        He stops.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        Come on. Leave the room.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Exit the specimen bay.|Specimen Bay Exit]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Archive Bay Entry [nobr missing-features] {"position":"1800,500","size":"100,200"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "archiveBayEntry">>
        You stand at the entrance of a large open space filled with fragmented projections. Visuals flicker between scenes of Earth — wars, cities, people, scientific breakthroughs.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        There it is. This one usually resides in this bay — possibly an astronomer or cartographer of some sorts.
Go talk to it. Don't you just wonder what's beneath its icy skin? Well, crystals or.. icicles..? Look, just make it 'think' using your intrinsic human perspective and give it a reality check, that should let you in on its 'mental state'. If you trust the process you will have felt something.. just something.
Since this one deals in orbit, topography and social studies, it would make sense to go along with those lines?
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        It already got here. But this is its designated place and it shouldn't scurry off anywhere for now.
Go talk to it. Don't you just wonder what's beneath its icy skin? Well, crystals or.. icicles..? Look, just make it 'think' using your intrinsic human perspective and give it a reality check. If you trust the process you will have felt something.
Since this one deals in orbit, topography and social studies, it would make sense to go along with those lines?
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        Well now you know what to do I guess. You're free to take it as a challenge though — gather the signals and get to the door. Very innovative I know.
Oh yeah, good luck by the way.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You're a smooth sailor. Just letting you know.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        An archive terminal glows to the right. The Cartographer is already present. The Exception stands further back, overlooking something beyond the projection field.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Read the archive terminal.|Archive Terminal]]
            [["What about that other one?" — approach the Exception.|Archive Exception]]
            [[Approach the Cartographer.|Cartographer Main]]
            [[Check the discarded room.|Discarded Room First]]
        </div>
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Archive Bay Return [nobr] {"position":"2400,500","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "archiveBayReturn">>
    	The archive bay again. The projections still flicker between scenes of Earth. The Cartographer has moved on. The Exception is no longer at the window. The view toward Earth is interactable
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You're looking at it again. The window that isn't a window. I know that view. I have spent quite a lot of time not looking at it, which is almost the same thing.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Look at the Earth.|Archive Earth View]]
            [[Check the discarded room.|Discarded Room First]]
            [[Return to the central room.|Pre Control Room]]
        </div>
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Archive Earth View [nobr] {"position":"2500,500","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "archiveEarthView">>
    	You look at Earth. No dialogue options. Just the planet, hanging there. A long beat. Then the narrator, quieter than usual.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        It's strange, isn't it. How much you can love something that has no idea you're looking at it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You know.. I used to think the problem with humanity was the scale. That humanity cared too much about things too small — the patch of ground, the particular people, the specific version of how things should go. But do you see any of that from here clearly? Not yet. Humanity's true strength lies in the abilities it developed around the neolithic campfires — belonging to a community, participating in real, human and dynamic interaction. Humans aren't hive-minded bees, ants, or especially these aliens. Why else would even within a civilisation there be nationalities and distinct groups?
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You see that in the middle? The ever-constructing global capital. The supposed monument to unity and human greatness. From up here it looks like what it is — an apple that's been eaten from the inside. The skin has to look good. There have to be enough healthy parts for people to risk a bite. If it sells, that's all that matters to the ones holding it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        The aliens have been around for decades and the supposed 'first and only' global government is still in observation mode as much as this ship you're on is. Because humanity doesn't bite hardest when it's aligned. It bites hardest when it's numerous and contradictory and acting from a hundred different angles at once. The campfire, not the capital.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        I've been up here long enough to know that. Long enough that knowing it isn't nearly enough anymore.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        A long pause. Then, almost to himself.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You know it's time to get moving when I've said too much.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Return to the central room.|Pre Control Room]]
            [[Check the discarded room.|Discarded Room First]]
        </div>
  <</ctp>>
</div>



:: Archive Exception [nobr agent missing-features] {"position":"1800,800","size":"100,100"}
<<script>>
            function addAgent(name) {
